<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>What is Privileged Access Management (PAM)?</h4>
<p>PAM secures and manages accounts with elevated permissions (admin, root, service accounts). CyberArk vaults credentials, controls access, monitors sessions, and enforces least privilege to prevent credential theft and insider threats.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>How does CyberArk prevent credential theft?</h4>
<p>CyberArk stores credentials in an encrypted vault, rotates passwords automatically, requires just-in-time access approval, records privileged sessions, and detects anomalous behavior using AI—preventing attackers from stealing and reusing credentials.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>What's the difference between PAM and EPM?</h4>
<p>PAM (Privileged Access Management) secures admin accounts. EPM (Endpoint Privilege Management) removes admin rights from endpoints and elevates only when needed. CyberArk offers both to implement comprehensive least privilege.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>Is CyberArk cloud-based or on-premise?</h4>
<p>Both. CyberArk Privilege Cloud is SaaS. Self-Hosted allows on-premise or private cloud deployment. Many enterprises use hybrid—cloud management with on-premise vaults for sensitive credentials.</p>
</div>
<div class="feature-card" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-md);">
<h4>How long does CyberArk implementation take?</h4>
<p>Typical enterprise deployments take 3-9 months depending on scope, number of systems, and organizational complexity. Phased rollouts starting with critical systems are recommended to demonstrate value early.</p>
</div>
